  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nA Rex block comprised of expansive ridging/high pressure over \nthe Great Lakes, eastern Ontario, and southwestern Quebec and a \ndeep coastal low/nor'easter just off the Mid-Atlantic coast will\nlead to tranquil conditions here locally over the weekend and \ninto early next week. Seasonable temperatures for late September\nwith highs in the mid 60s to mid 70s and lows in the mid 40s to\nmid 50s can be expected while the blocking pattern is in place \nand keeps the same air mass locked in over the region.\n\nOne prominent shortwave presently over the central Plains will \nattempt to lift northeastward into the western Great Lakes \ntonight but will be stymied as it encounters the aforementioned \nGreat Lakes ridge. A steady feed of dry low-mid-level air along \nthe southern flank of the ridge should erode the associated \nshower activity as it attempts to enter our forecast area while \nits main forcing source is quickly weakened. A few of the latest\nhigh-resolution model runs suggest that these showers/sprinkles\ncould get as far east as the I-39 corridor or so late tonight \ninto Sunday morning before fading for good, though the majority \nof the latest forecast guidance generally favors this activity \nstaying fenced off just to the west of our westernmost counties.\n\nBy midweek, the eastern CONUS Rex block will have broken down, \nallowing for a more progressive weather pattern to take shape. A\ndeep, positively-tilted longwave Pacific trough will evolve \ninto a cut-off upper low over the Desert Southwest Monday into \nTuesday, while a separate shortwave component of this trough \nbreaks off and continues to progress towards the Great Lakes. As\nthis shortwave disturbance arrives in the region in tandem with\na frontal zone and an influx of column moisture, a period of \nrain appears likely to occur in our area sometime late Tuesday \ninto Wednesday. There is a fair amount of ensemble support for \na modest amount of instability building into our area during\nthis time period, suggesting that there could be some potential\nfor thunderstorms. However, this support is far from unanimous \nat this point.\n\nHow things will play out for late Wednesday and beyond will \nhinge heavily upon a complex interaction between the \naforementioned upper low and the remnants of Pacific Hurricane \nPolo. Ensemble guidance broadly favors another period of rain \n(or perhaps just one, drawn-out period of rainfall beginning \nwith the late Tuesday/Wednesday precipitation) occurring here \nduring the latter half of the week as these atmospheric features\nlift northeastward towards the Midwest and induce a poleward \nsurge of combined Gulf and Pacific moisture. Unsurprisingly \nthough, there is still quite a bit of spread across the latest \nglobal ensemble suites in exactly when any additional rainfall \nmay occur here and how much rain we'll get. The broad-brushed \nchance and lower-end likely PoPs offered up by the NBM for the \nfirst few days of October thus seem to look appropriate for \nnow.\n\nOgorek",
